WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
Warm pentan-1-ol with ethanoic acid and a little

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:07.360
concentrated sulfuric acid, and you make pentyl ethanoate,

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.680
an ester that smells like bananas.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.920
A class makes it and gets about sixty

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.800
per cent of the most it could make.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.920
Is that a failed practical?

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.360
Not necessarily.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.360
Let's see what limits the yield, and how

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.600
to test fairly whether you can raise it.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.840
The aim: to find out whether excess ethanoic

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.160
acid increases the yield of pentyl ethanoate.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.680
Some groups mix the acid and alcohol one

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:32.480
to one.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.960
Others use twice as much acid.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.200
They react one to one, so the alcohol

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.920
sets the most ester any group could make,

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.560
and every yield is measured against it.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.120
From the esters video, you'd predict the excess

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.960
pushes the equilibrium right.

00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:51.206
Measure the pentan-1-ol and the ethanoic acid into

00:00:51.247 --> 00:00:54.406
a round-bottom flask, and add a few anti-bumping

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:55.366
granules.

00:00:55.687 --> 00:00:57.766
Your teacher then adds about ten drops of

00:00:57.927 --> 00:01:00.966
concentrated sulfuric acid, the catalyst.

00:01:01.287 --> 00:01:03.686
Fit the condenser upright, and leave its top

00:01:03.847 --> 00:01:04.526
open.

00:01:04.807 --> 00:01:07.526
This mixture boils well above a hundred degrees,

00:01:07.607 --> 00:01:09.846
so a water bath isn't hot enough.

00:01:10.167 --> 00:01:12.086
Heat it with an electric heating mantle for

00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:13.446
about forty minutes.

00:01:13.927 --> 00:01:16.326
The condenser sends the vapours back, so they

00:01:16.407 --> 00:01:17.286
don't escape.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.580
What changes, and what stays the same?

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.580
The independent variable is the mole ratio of

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:27.540
acid to alcohol: one to one, or two

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:28.260
to one.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.060
The dependent variable is the percentage yield.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.340
Keep the amount of pentan-1-ol, the catalyst, the

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.540
heating time, and every work-up step the same.

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:41.646
After cooling, pour the mixture into a separating

00:01:41.807 --> 00:01:43.406
funnel with cold water.

00:01:43.727 --> 00:01:46.366
The ester barely dissolves, so it forms its

00:01:46.447 --> 00:01:49.006
own layer: on top here, because it's less

00:01:49.087 --> 00:01:50.526
dense than water.

00:01:50.567 --> 00:01:53.326
Wash it with sodium hydrogen carbonate solution to

00:01:53.527 --> 00:01:56.206
remove the leftover acid, and vent the funnel

00:01:56.407 --> 00:01:58.846
often, away from people, to release the carbon

00:01:58.927 --> 00:02:00.126
dioxide.

00:02:00.327 --> 00:02:03.886
Then dry the ester with anhydrous magnesium sulfate,

00:02:04.007 --> 00:02:05.966
pour it into a weighed vial, and weigh

00:02:06.047 --> 00:02:06.366
it.

00:02:07.567 --> 00:02:09.806
Now the yield, for one two to one

00:02:09.887 --> 00:02:10.286
group.

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:13.886
Four point four one grams of pentan-1-ol, divided

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:16.606
by its molar mass, is zero point zero

00:02:16.847 --> 00:02:18.886
five zero zero moles.

00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:21.326
One mole of alcohol makes one mole of

00:02:21.527 --> 00:02:24.286
ester, so that's also the most ester they

00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:24.846
could make.

00:02:25.327 --> 00:02:28.046
Times the ester's molar mass, that's six point

00:02:28.127 --> 00:02:29.166
five one grams.

00:02:29.647 --> 00:02:32.366
They collected three point nine zero grams.

00:02:32.687 --> 00:02:36.046
Divide, times a hundred: about sixty per cent.

00:02:37.267 --> 00:02:38.866
So where did the rest go?

00:02:39.427 --> 00:02:41.026
First, equilibrium.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:44.466
The reaction stops short, so even with excess

00:02:44.627 --> 00:02:47.746
acid, some alcohol never becomes ester.

00:02:47.827 --> 00:02:48.706
That's the ceiling.

00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:50.866
Second, losses.

00:02:51.107 --> 00:02:53.586
Some ester stays on the glassware, some goes

00:02:53.627 --> 00:02:55.586
in the washes, and some is left on

00:02:55.627 --> 00:02:56.866
the drying agent.

00:02:57.027 --> 00:02:59.346
Those losses take the result lower still.

00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:02.406
Three groups ran each ratio.

00:03:02.487 --> 00:03:04.886
One to one gave about forty per cent

00:03:04.967 --> 00:03:08.166
each time, and two to one about sixty.

00:03:08.247 --> 00:03:10.646
Within each ratio, the results agree within a

00:03:10.727 --> 00:03:12.646
few per cent, so they're reliable.

00:03:13.127 --> 00:03:15.526
And the gap between the ratios, about twenty

00:03:15.687 --> 00:03:18.566
points, is much bigger than that spread.

00:03:18.687 --> 00:03:21.366
So, in these runs, excess acid gave a

00:03:21.527 --> 00:03:22.486
higher yield.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.540
Is sixty per cent accurate?

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:28.820
If the ester was dry and pure, yes:

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.820
that's the yield they really got.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.260
It's low because ester was lost, not mismeasured,

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.580
and repeats can't bring it back.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.380
Water or leftover acid would do the opposite,

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.380
making the mass too high.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.620
And is the comparison valid?

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.340
Matching the work-up helps, but more acid can

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.060
change the washes, and forty minutes may not

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:50.500
reach equilibrium.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:52.980
So the data support a higher yield with

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:55.460
excess acid, under these conditions.

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:58.266
Is it really the ester?

00:03:58.507 --> 00:04:01.386
It forms its own layer, and after washing,

00:04:01.467 --> 00:04:04.586
no visible fizz with hydrogen carbonate suggests little

00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:05.946
acid is left.

00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:08.906
Checks like these support it, but none proves

00:04:08.947 --> 00:04:09.546
it alone.

00:04:10.027 --> 00:04:12.106
If your teacher allows it, waft a little

00:04:12.147 --> 00:04:14.506
of the smell towards you, but never sniff

00:04:14.547 --> 00:04:15.386
it directly.

00:04:16.601 --> 00:04:17.880
Now the risks.

00:04:18.121 --> 00:04:22.120
Concentrated sulfuric acid causes severe burns, so your

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:24.760
teacher adds it, and you wear safety glasses,

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:26.480
gloves and a lab coat.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.200
Glacial ethanoic acid is corrosive and flammable.

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:33.400
Pentan-1-ol and the ester are flammable too, so

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.520
no flames: use the heating mantle, and keep

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:38.200
the condenser top open.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:40.680
And collect the organic residues in the labelled

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.080
organic waste, not the sink.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.740
One line to keep.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:49.740
Equilibrium sets the ceiling; the work-up decides how

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:50.780
close you get.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:52.920
Your turn.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.320
A student refluxes three point seven zero grams

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.600
of butan-1-ol with excess ethanoic acid and a

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:01.600
sulfuric acid catalyst.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.640
After washing and drying, she collects three point

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.040
one nine grams of butyl ethanoate.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.720
Calculate the percentage yield, and explain two reasons

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.680
it's less than a hundred per cent.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:14.320
Pause here to answer.

00:05:24.734 --> 00:05:27.533
Three point seven zero over seventy four point

00:05:27.694 --> 00:05:30.813
one two is zero point zero four nine

00:05:30.934 --> 00:05:34.093
nine moles of alcohol, so at most that

00:05:34.174 --> 00:05:37.213
much ester: five point eight zero grams.

00:05:37.534 --> 00:05:39.933
Three point one nine over five point eight

00:05:40.014 --> 00:05:42.653
zero is about fifty five per cent.

00:05:42.694 --> 00:05:45.773
It's lower because the reaction stops at equilibrium,

00:05:45.854 --> 00:05:48.253
and ester is lost in the washes.

00:05:48.294 --> 00:05:50.653
The trap is dividing the masses directly.

00:05:51.867 --> 00:05:52.506
Last one.

00:05:52.747 --> 00:05:55.266
A student makes propyl ethanoate.

00:05:55.467 --> 00:05:57.466
She runs off the water layer and weighs

00:05:57.507 --> 00:06:00.346
the ester layer straight away, without washing or

00:06:00.427 --> 00:06:01.066
drying it.

00:06:01.547 --> 00:06:03.626
Her percentage yield comes out at a hundred

00:06:03.747 --> 00:06:04.586
and four per cent.

00:06:05.227 --> 00:06:08.266
Explain why her result is inaccurate, and describe

00:06:08.347 --> 00:06:10.146
two changes to her method.

00:06:10.427 --> 00:06:11.706
Pause here to answer.

00:06:22.134 --> 00:06:24.373
Over a hundred per cent means she weighed

00:06:24.454 --> 00:06:28.133
things that aren't ester: water, and probably leftover

00:06:28.214 --> 00:06:30.693
acid and alcohol, so the yield is too

00:06:30.774 --> 00:06:31.493
high.

00:06:31.814 --> 00:06:33.893
She should wash the layer with sodium hydrogen

00:06:34.054 --> 00:06:37.413
carbonate solution, and dry it with anhydrous magnesium

00:06:37.574 --> 00:06:39.493
sulfate before weighing.

00:06:39.974 --> 00:06:42.133
The trap is saying it went to completion.

00:06:42.534 --> 00:06:44.773
Even then, the most is a hundred per

00:06:44.854 --> 00:06:45.333
cent.

00:06:46.534 --> 00:06:50.293
So: reflux to approach equilibrium, wash and dry

00:06:50.374 --> 00:06:52.533
to get the ester out, and calculate the

00:06:52.614 --> 00:06:54.853
yield from moles of the limiting reactant.

00:06:55.414 --> 00:06:58.293
Equilibrium caps the yield, and losses take it

00:06:58.374 --> 00:06:58.853
lower.

00:06:59.414 --> 00:07:02.373
To compare conditions fairly, match the work-up, and

00:07:02.454 --> 00:07:04.693
check each product is dry and pure.

00:07:05.974 --> 00:07:08.133
Next: amines and amides.

00:07:09.334 --> 00:07:10.773
Here are the key notes.

00:07:11.014 --> 00:07:12.933
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:13.014 --> 00:07:13.813
to copy them.
